{{also|Rouse|rouše}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹaʊz/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-rouse.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{homophones|en|rows}} {{qualifier|noisy arguments}}
* {{rhymes|en|aʊz|s=1}}
<!-- 1 syllable words !-->

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|rousen}}, from {{der|en|xno|reuser}}, {{m|fro|ruser}}, originally used in English of hawks shaking the feathers of the body, from {{der|en|la|recūsō}}, by loss of the medial 'c.' {{doublet|en|recuse}}.

Figurative meaning {{m-g|to stir up, provoke to activity}} is from 1580s; that of {{m-g|awaken}} is first recorded 1590s.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|en|rouze||obsolete}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An [[arousal]].
# {{lb|en|military|British|and|Canada}} The [[sounding]] of a [[bugle]] in the morning after [[reveille]], to [[signal]] that soldiers are to [[rise]] from [[bed]], often ''the rouse''.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[wake]] (someone) from [[sleep]], or from [[apathy]].
#: {{syn|en|bring round|roust|wake up|Thesaurus:awaken}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=January|author=David L. Smith|title=A Runaway at Beattock|journal=Railway Magazine|page=53|text=John Hedley was Locomotive Foreman at Beattock. He was in bed, but they '''roused''' him, and he gave orders for one of his pilot engines to go up to the summit, get Mitchell's train, and take it to Carlisle.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1979|author=w:Bernard Malamud|title=w:Dubin's Lives|location=New York|publisher=Farrar Straus Giroux|chapter=Eight|page=284|url=https://archive.org/details/dubinslives00mala|passage=Dubin slept through the ringing alarm, aware of Kitty trying to '''rouse''' him and then letting him sleep.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[awoken]] from [[sleep]], or from [[apathy]].
#: {{syn|en|arise|get up|wake up|Thesaurus:wake}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|III|2|text=Good things of day begin to droop and drowse;<br>Night’s black agents to their preys do '''rouse'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1687|author=w:Francis Atterbury|title=An Answer to Some Considerations on the Spirit of Martin Luther|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A26149.0001.001|pages=41–42|publisher=Oxford
|passage=As for the heat, with which he treated his other adversaries, ’twas sometimes strain’d a little too far, but in the general was extremely well fitted by the Providence of God to '''rowse''' up a people, the most phlegmatic of any in Christendome.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1713|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=Ode for Musick|location=London|publisher=Bernard Lintott|section=stanza 2, p. 3|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004809282.0001.000
|passage=At Musick, ''Melancholy'' lifts her Head;<br>Dull ''{{w|Morpheus}}'' '''rowzes''' from his Bed;}}
# To [[cause]], [[stir up]], [[excite]] (a feeling, thought, etc.).
#: {{syn|en|galvanise|stoke|Thesaurus:thrill}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''rouse''' the faculties, passions, or emotions}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe 2|page=127|text={{...}} their first Step in Dangers, after the common Efforts are over, was always to despair, lie down under it, and die, without '''rousing''' their Thoughts up to proper Remedies for Escape.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author=w:Anne Brontë|title=w:The Tenant of Wildfell Hall|location=London|publisher=John Murray|year_published=1900|chapter=27|pageurl=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/969/969-h/969-h.htm
|passage=‘You may think it all very fine, Mr. Huntingdon, to amuse yourself with '''rousing''' my jealousy; but take care you don’t '''rouse''' my hate instead.  And when you have once extinguished my love, you will find it no easy matter to kindle it again.’}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1961|author=w:V. S. Naipaul|title=w:A House for Mr Biswas|publisher=Penguin|year_published=1992|section=Part Two, Chapter 5, p. 494|url=https://archive.org/details/houseformrbiswas00vsna_1
|passage={{...}} he had grown to look upon houses as things that concerned other people, like churches, butchers’ stalls, cricket matches and football matches. They had ceased to '''rouse''' ambition or misery. He had lost the vision of the house.}}
# {{senseid|en|provoke}} To [[provoke]] (someone) to [[action]] or [[anger]].
#: {{syn|en|inflame|set off|Thesaurus:incite||Thesaurus:enrage}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=2|line=284-287|text=He scarce had finisht, when such murmur filld<br>Th’ Assembly, as when hollow Rocks retain<br>The sound of blustring winds, which all night long<br>Had '''rous’d''' the Sea {{...}}|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Persuasion|12|text=“A surgeon!” said Anne.<br>He caught the word; it seemed to '''rouse''' him at once, and saying only—“True, true, a surgeon this instant,” was darting away, when Anne eagerly suggested—<br>“Captain Benwick, would not it be better for Captain Benwick? {{...}}”}}
#* {{RQ:Faulkner Light in August|chapter=12|page=254|passage=He tried to argue with her. But it was like trying to argue with a tree: she did not even '''rouse''' herself to deny, she just listened quietly and then talked again in that level, cold tone as if he had never spoken.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1980|author=w:J. M. Coetzee|title=w:Waiting for the Barbarians|url=https://archive.org/details/waitingforbarbar00coet|page=108|publisher=Penguin|year_published=1982
|passage=The words they stopped me from uttering may have been very paltry indeed, hardly words to '''rouse''' the rabble.}}
# To cause to start from a [[covert]] or lurking place.
#: {{ux|en|to '''rouse''' a deer or other animal of the chase}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|2|11|page=350|text=Deformed creatures, in straunge difference,<br>Some hauing heads like Harts, some like to Snakes,<br>Some like wilde Bores late '''rouzd''' out of the brakes,}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|III|3|text=Hark, the game is '''roused'''!}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Windsor Forest|page=7<!--|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004809340.0001.000-->|text=The Youth rush eager to the Sylvan War;<br>Swarm o’er the Lawns, the Forest Walks surround,<br>'''Rowze''' the fleet Hart, and chear the opening Hound.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To [[pull]] by main [[strength]]; to [[haul]].
#* {{RQ:Marryat Newton Forster|volume=1|chapter=5|page=71|text=Tom, you and the boy '''rouse''' the cable up—get about ten fathoms on deck, and bend it.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To raise; to make erect.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|1|11|page=157|text=And ouer, all with brasen scales was armd,<br>Like plated cote of steele, so couched neare,<br>That nought mote perce, ne might his corse bee harmd<br>With dint of swerd, nor push of pointed speare,<br>Which as an Eagle, seeing pray appeare,<br>His aery plumes doth '''rouze''', full rudely dight,<br>So shaked he, that horror was to heare,<br>For as the clashing of an Armor bright,<br>Such noyse his '''rouzed''' scales did send vnto the knight.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|IV|3|text=He that outlives this day, and comes safe home,<br>Will stand a tip-toe when the day is named,<br>And '''rouse''' him at the name of Crispian.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|when followed by "on"}} To [[tell off]]; to [[criticise]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''roused''' on her for being late yet again.}}

